so the headlines read that remington is  subpoenaing the student records of the  first graders who were shot and killed  in the sandy hook massacre so i assume  that in this litigation remington issued  a very broad discovery request something  along the lines of all documents related  to the children and someone blew it out  of the proportion by saying oh they're  specifically requesting educational  records which have no relevance actually  not so and the headlines are actually  pretty accurate here i took a look at  the actual discovery requests which read  any and all educational records in your  possession including but not limited to  application admission paperwork  attendance records transcripts report  cards disciplinary records  correspondence and any and all other  educational information and records  pertaining to each of the five first  graders at issue so yeah those are  actually much more specific than i  thought and it's hard to see what kind  of relevance those documents would have  in this particular litigation and so as  a result the plaintiff's lawyers are  trying to get a protective order to  prevent the disclosure of those records  related to the first grade children who  were killed in that case and they  probably stand a pretty good chance of  quashing that subpoena but we'll see  what happens in court
 

